  "question": "If $y = \\sqrt{\\sin x + \\sqrt{\\sin x + \\sqrt{\\sin x + ...}}}$, prove that $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{\\cos x}{2y - 1}$.",
  "answeroption": "B",
  "explanation": "Let $y=\\sqrt{\\sin x+y}$<br /><br />Square:<br />$y^2=\\sin x+y$<br /><br />Differentiate:<br />$2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\cos x+\\frac{dy}{dx}$<br /><br />$\\frac{dy}{dx}(2y-1)=\\cos x$<br /><br />$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\cos x}{2y-1}$.",
